WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.960
If, for some reason, you ever find yourself&nbsp;
reading a bunch of papers about cryptomonads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.187
you might come across this strange&nbsp;
fact: they have four genomes.

00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:14.440
That sounds like a lot of genomes.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.960
But what exactly does that even mean?

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.405
And what does the cryptomonas&nbsp;
do with all those genomes?

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.680
The genus Cryptomonas was introduced in 1831&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.680
by the prolific microbiologist&nbsp;
Christian Gottfried Ehrenberg.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.640
They’re mostly greenish brown&nbsp;
in color, and in nature, green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.120
is often associated with photosynthesis&nbsp;
and the various pigments that drive it.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.320
And many cryptomonads are photosynthetic,&nbsp;
using chlorophyll and accessory pigments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.560
are packaged in photosynthetic&nbsp;
organelles called plastids.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:05.360
But there are also several cryptomonad species&nbsp;
that are no photosynthetic, like the colorless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.080
cryptomonas paramecium frantically dancing&nbsp;
around the giant blepharisma in this clip.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.200
You might expect that this means the&nbsp;
Cryptomonas paramecium doesn’t have plastids.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:22.438
It isn’t performing photosynthesis, so what need&nbsp;
would it have for a photosynthetic organelle?

00:01:22.438 --> 00:01:23.071
Well...

00:01:23.071 --> 00:01:24.840
Plenty, it turns out.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:31.040
To understand why, we've got to look into how&nbsp;
the cryptomonas got its plastid to begin with.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.510
In some ways, it’s a standard tale&nbsp;
of endosymbiosis in the microcosmos.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.800
At some point in the past, an ancestor&nbsp;
of cryptomonas consumed a red algae.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:49.480
But instead of fully digesting it for food,&nbsp;
the organism kept bits of the red algae around—

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.000
namely, the algae’s plastid.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.800
Interestingly though, the red algae’s plastid&nbsp;
is itself the product of endosymbiosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:02.640
a similar tale involving some ancient&nbsp;
ancestor, only it had likely consumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.400
a free-living cyanobacteria that it&nbsp;
eventually converted into a plastid.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.360
So the red algae plastids are the product&nbsp;
of what’s called primary endosymbiosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.840
and cryptomonad plastids are the result&nbsp;
of what’s called secondary endosymbiosis.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:24.880
And on top of coopting the red algae’s plastid,&nbsp;
the cryptomonas ancestor also took the red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.840
algae’s purple pigment phycoerythrin&nbsp;
to power the photosynthesis.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.480
But plastids are not the only example of&nbsp;
endosymbiosis contained in cryptomonads.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.560
The other one is a trait that&nbsp;
cryptomonads share with us and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.640
almost all other eukaryotes: the mitochondrion.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.720
And this is why the cryptomonad has four genomes.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:52.520
The first is its own nuclear DNA,&nbsp;
the foundation of its identity.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:59.080
The second is its mitochondrial DNA, a remnant&nbsp;
of DNA of an essential energy-producing organelle.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.760
The third is the plastid DNA from the red algae.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.600
And the fourth?

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.560
The fourth sequence of DNA is a weird one.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:14.840
It’s called the nucleomorph, and it’s like a&nbsp;
version of the red algae’s nucleus but just…less.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.600
This makes cryptomonads different from&nbsp;
some other eukaryotes that have developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.440
organelles through secondary endosymbiosis&nbsp;
because those other organisms have managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:28.880
to lose the nuclear remains of&nbsp;
their endosymbiont altogether.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.600
And different is exciting when you are a scientist.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.840
In this case, it makes cryptomonads useful&nbsp;
to those who want to learn more about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:44.560
diverse paths endosymbiosis takes as&nbsp;
it converts organisms into organelles.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.640
Returning to our Cryptomonas paramecium, for&nbsp;
example, scientists have looked into why the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:55.120
species would keep plastids around when&nbsp;
they don’t use them for photosynthesis.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.440
But plastids are able to do more than&nbsp;
just photosynthesis, it turns out.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.680
At least, when scientists studied the plastids&nbsp;
in Cryptomonas paramecium and several other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.160
non-photosynthetic cryptomonads, they&nbsp;
found that the plastids were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:15.440
make fatty acids and amino acids and carry&nbsp;
out a number of other important reactions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.480
though actual functions a plastid might&nbsp;
serve can vary from species to species.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.840
Despite their intrigue, cryptomonads are&nbsp;
also challenging to study for a few reasons.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.120
For one, they can be a challenge&nbsp;
to collect and keep alive.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.600
James, our master of microscopes, said&nbsp;
that they tend to die very quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.720
seeming to disintegrate as soon as he&nbsp;
looks at them under the microscope—

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.080
though he has found that the&nbsp;
non-photosynthetic ones are a little hardier.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.520
But in addition to the difficulty&nbsp;
of keeping cryptomonads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.880
they’re also really hard to tell&nbsp;
apart using light microscopy.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.680
The features that distinguish species are&nbsp;
just too muddied and difficult to categorize.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.600
But microbiologists are resourceful,&nbsp;
and since the end of the 19th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:09.200
they’ve been working to isolate and maintain pure&nbsp;
cultures of algae that can provide scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:15.080
with the uncontaminated specimens they need&nbsp;
to better understand the microbial world.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:19.640
One of these scientists was Ernst&nbsp;
Georg Pringsheim, a German scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:24.720
who first began publishing papers&nbsp;
on how to culture algae in 1912.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.560
He refined techniques that allowed him&nbsp;
to pick up single cells with a pipette,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:34.640
and figured out the quality of water that&nbsp;
would best allow his cultures to survive.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:41.920
In 1922, he had moved to Prague to carry on his&nbsp;
work as professor and develop his algal cultures.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.840
And while there, he met Olga Zimmerman, a student&nbsp;
who would become his frequent collaborator.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.600
Pringsheim was offered a position that&nbsp;
would allow him to return to Germany.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.240
But he was Jewish, and&nbsp;
Hitler was coming into power.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:00.520
So instead Pringsheim and his family&nbsp;
left Prague for England in 1938,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.080
right before the German&nbsp;
occupation of Czechoslovakia.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.920
And even with the turbulence of war around him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:11.280
Pringsheim brought some of his&nbsp;
algae strains with him to England.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.160
And he maintained them, keeping them&nbsp;
going so that when he did return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:20.880
Germany in 1953, he could use them&nbsp;
to establish cultures there as well.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.160
In fact, even after his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.840
Pringsheim’s cultures have served as the&nbsp;
basis for more than 400 algae cultures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:33.920
distributed across collections maintained&nbsp;
at various institutions around the world.

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:38.572
And they’re still being used for research and&nbsp;
teaching today.

00:06:38.572 --> 00:06:40.080
Just this year, a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.160
scientists reported the results of their work&nbsp;
studying a particular strain of cryptomonads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:52.800
whose origins lie in a Pringsheim culture that&nbsp;
scientists have continued tending to for decades.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.160
When scientists looked inside this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:59.400
strain, they found two bacterial&nbsp;
endosymbionts and a bacteriophage—

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.680
or bacteria-infecting virus—

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.920
also living inside the Cryptomonads.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:09.520
Their work revealed an expanded&nbsp;
world within this particular strain.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:15.720
(And if you’re counting, this strain&nbsp;
of Cryptomonas has seven genomes)

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.320
We don’t know yet how the relationships&nbsp;
between these organisms work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:24.920
but the fact that this grouping&nbsp;
has persisted through around 4000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:30.640
generations of this strain suggests&nbsp;
that it is probably very stable.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.600
It is a little odd to 
describe the world within these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:39.040
strains as “stable” when the world&nbsp;
that produced them was anything but.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:44.760
It’s also hard to adequately place meaning&nbsp;
and value to Pringsheim’s commitment to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:50.320
cultures in the context of the antisemitism&nbsp;
and war that shaped his life and work.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:56.840
And yet it is a wonderful thing to leave&nbsp;
a legacy built on care and curiosity.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:02.280
It is a legacy that sustains itself by&nbsp;
training the next generation in its methods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:05.280
and providing the vessel for new questions.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.960
It leaves a mark on this world, as enduring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:14.468
you might say, as an ancient&nbsp;
genome buried in a cryptomonad.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:25.760
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

